WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100 align:start position:0%
 
thank you very much for being with us  and can you just explain how and why you  decided to quit the government yeah we  were heading towards these votes on  alternatives I'd been a very strong  supporter of the primaries withdrawal  agreement and I continue to support it  and I voted for it last Friday Minister  or not but I became very concerned that  if it didn't get through we needed an  alternative and my colleagues had to be  free to vote for that alternative  otherwise a parliamentary vote on an  alternative didn't mean very much and I  wasn't certain that would be the case I  wasn't certain that Oliver lemons  amendment would get through and I felt I  had to vote for it and I was also  conscious that other of my colleagues  were doing the work to create  alternatives and I wasn't and I felt it  was time to to do that but I still want  to see the withdrawal agreement go  through but if not because I'm concerned  about No Deal I want to make sure  there's an alternative available from  your conversations with the Prime  Minister and you've had lots of them I  mean you went to checkers the day before  you resigned and to talk with the Prime  Minister and you worried that she might  try and go for a no deal brexit if her  deal doesn't get through the perhaps has  always been pretty clear that that  really isn't her option she's very  concerned about the State of the Union  and what a No Deal brexit would mean in  relation to that not least in relation  to Ireland and she's been pretty clear  about that however I'm also very  conscious that she's under huge pressure  from some in the cabinet and others who  see no deal as the the way for the  United Kingdom to leave and the Prime  Minister needs to know that there are  colleagues who don't agree with that the  colleagues who were prepared to be as  committed as some of our No Deal  colleagues to prevent this and that we  have to find the compromise I voted to  remain in the European Union I'm not a  remainer now I spent my time in the  house command since the referendum  voting to leave because I think that's  the right thing for the United Kingdom  so I've compromised to a degree and I've  said this to my colleagues we're looking  for more compromise from them if the  country is going to get through this  so do you think there are other members  the cabinet are the ministers who are  willing to resign from the government to  try and stop a new deal how many do you  think there are there are I'm not going  to put any number on it but that really  shouldn't be in a way what we're talking  about resigning from the government is  not a it's not a great thing to do and  I'm sorry that I had to do it there's  been a lot of people but ultimately you  have to have a government it's got to  keep functioning it's got to keep  running the country and we're doing a  lot of things besides concentrating on  brexit I don't want to see more  resignations my colleagues and I want to  see a situation in which we've made an  agreement and and we've done what we  think the British people have wanted to  do which is leave the EU but leave on  good terms these are our partners these  are our colleagues and friends in the  European Union the way the debate is  gone has been to separate us from our  our partners and turn them into our  enemies and we we are at peril from this  I spent enough time in conflict areas  over the last few years to know how  fragile peace and security is you can  pull a thread  and before you know where you are things  have got very very difficult we shall be  looking to partner with our friends  leave on good terms and leave with good  terms for the future relationships and  that's what I hope colleagues will still  compromise on doing I don't want to see  more resignations I mean talking about  the fragility there of nations around  the world I mean are you really worried  about the future of the UK if people  feel that brexit has gone wrong and I do  worry  I think like many colleagues and like  both Lisa and Emily have spoken about on  the program before there's a greater  polarization in politics than ever  before and that polarization has turned  not into debate where you recognize the  strength of an opponent's argument and  you move to overcome it but you start to  feel there's nothing whatsoever good in  what someone else is putting to you and  that's very dangerous and we've seen in  in other countries that very quickly if  people become so polarized they can't  accept an argument on the other side you  know people start to resort to different  methods now it hasn't happened here and  when I've been going around the world  although people are puzzled about us  leaving Europe they have commented about  the strength of our democracy and how  we've been able to have these arguments  but I'm very conscious and one of the  reasons I voted to remain in the  European Union was we've had you know  seventy years of peace since the end of  the Second World War in Europe and these  things are held together by  multinational relationships and  institutions now if we're going to leave  the EU and it's perfectly possible to do  so without everything fracturing we  should do it in a way that ensures that  relationships continue and continue to  be strong and I think we have to be very  careful about how we do these things and  how we debate and argue with each other  I've been talking about the kind of  polarization of the debate the the lack  of ability of people to compromise don't  let grief your colleague who backs a  second referendum and lost a confidence  motion and his conservative Association  this week and you tweeted to say where  on earth are we heading with situations  like this where are we heading and I was  extremely disappointed with the  resulting in Dominic's constituency now  there appears to be a situation where  people have quotes rejoined quotes the  Conservative Party because they've been  belonging to another like you Kipp and  they're setting about some of my  colleagues I must say I don't notice the  reverse and conservatives who voted to  remain and not ganging up on colleagues  who have resolutely refused to to leave  or being the hardliners in order to  remove them there's no remain of force  getting together to seek these elections  we want a broad church in the  Conservative Party I want in it I value  colleagues like Jacob Riis Mark and  Steve Baker who have very strong  principles and work the other way I want  to be part of a Conservative Party that  includes those views and I hope they  want to be part of a Conservative Party  that includes outstanding individuals  like Dominic who have given great  service the country as Attorney General  and as Minister and we've got to work  very hard to find this way through if we  don't if our associations become more  polarized and mine has been terrific  with me it's recognized what I voted for  is recognized what I've been trying to  do  since that vote to ensure that United  Kingdom leaves the EU in a decent way  and most associations I think are  actually full of people like that and  we've got to make sure that the  Conservative Party stays broaden its  membership very broad in those who  support and vote for us which is why  we've got to get on with looking to the  future and start tackling other issues  that people feel are very important the  United Kingdom and keep as many people  on board as possible political parties  are Fran child too and we shouldn't  forget that they can vary it the things  can very easily fall apart we should  fight hard to keep the broad nature of  our political parties both the Labour  Party and Conservative Party for fear  that they become more fractured more  polarized which I don't think will do  the country any good and just finally  before we let you go and there's lots of  rumors in Westminster about the  possibility of a snap general election  if there is a general election should  Tories may lead the Conservative Party  into it well firstly I'm we're all with  Brenda from Bristol on this oh no not  another one I don't see a general  election now adding to the mix that were  in at the moment as anything that would  be likely to be helpful to the country  whoever leads the Conservative Party or  whoever leads the Labour Party or anyone  else at the moment we're not in a phase  for that we need to stick to to this  task that we have in relation to  European Union that settle that and then  move forward offer the public the wide  range of choices that political parties  do that's the best route forward not to  worry about an immediate general  election or who leads anybody let's get  on with doing this job in the first  place nicely dodged should Therese may  lead the party into the general election  the Prime Minister has already said that  she's expecting to leave after we get  the the first stage of brexit going  through I don't anticipate a general  election before that is done okay so  probably the answer would be no

